      {
        "id": 56616,
        "name": "pppery",
        "username": "pppery",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/p/3d9bf3/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-03-31T12:47:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"tacticaltechkyle\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/t/d2c977/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> tacticaltechkyle:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I think it makes things in general a lot less messy when static effects (like all damage modifications including immunities) are calculated before conditional effects.If AbZ is immune to damage, the conditional effect of NPCU would (in my eyes) happen after the static immunity is taken into account, meaning he would be dealing himself in essence 0 damage, then instead of taking that 0 damage, he converts it to that much healing, in this case 0.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>does this mean that if something was increasing his healing, zero ciould heal from the buff? i think \"would be dealt damage\" doesn't trigger when the damage is 0.</p>",
